# The monetary store itself. Hundreds of gigabytes of derived block records.
# Nobody should fetch this from a git remote: the whole point is that you
# re-derive it from your own node and compare the commitment.
store/
mstore/
*.dat
*.jsonl

# The offset and block indexes. Rebuilt on demand, large, machine-specific.
.txq_offsets
txq_*.idx
*.idx
electrs-db/

# NOT ignored: results/
#
# The README calls results/ "the evidence behind every number on this page",
# and says that if a figure disagrees with a log, the log is right. Ignoring
# it would quietly remove the project's own evidence from the repository.
# Logs are small text files; they belong in git.
#
# Service logs are a different thing — machine-specific, noisy, and they
# carry paths and peer counts nobody else needs.
monetary-ui.log
monetary-daemon.log
debug.log

# State. Carries this node's height, hash and commitment — reproducible by
# anyone running the tools, and misleading if shipped as though it were
# canonical.
state.json
recent.jsonl
races.jsonl

# Config. bitcoin.conf and monetary.conf hold paths, ports and a user agent
# specific to one machine; the backups hold earlier versions of the same.
bitcoin.conf
monetary.conf
*.bak.*
*.removed.*
.monetary/
.electrs/

# Anything resembling a credential. There should be none in this tree, and a
# pattern here is cheaper than noticing one in a published commit.
.cookie
*.pem
*.key
hostname
onion_*

# Python and editor noise.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.DS_Store
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/

# Build trees from patch_build.sh. Core's source is not this project's to
# redistribute, and it is gigabytes.
core-patched/
build/
